## 3. Code

```rust
use serde_json::json;
use yup_oauth2::{read_application_secret, InstalledFlowAuthenticator, InstalledFlowReturnMethod};

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    // OAuth: opens a browser the first time, then caches the token in token.json
    let secret = read_application_secret("credentials.json").await?;
    let auth = InstalledFlowAuthenticator::builder(
        secret,
        InstalledFlowReturnMethod::HTTPRedirect,
    )
    .persist_tokens_to_disk("token.json")
    .build()
    .await?;

    let scopes = &["https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.events"];
    let token = auth.token(scopes).await?;
    let access_token = token.token().ok_or("no access token returned")?;

    // 1pm every other Wednesday, starting Wed 2026-10-14
    let event = json!({
        "summary": "Biweekly Meeting",
        "description": "Created from Rust",
        "start": {
            "dateTime": "2026-10-14T13:00:00",
            "timeZone": "America/New_York"
        },
        "end": {
            "dateTime": "2026-10-14T14:00:00",
            "timeZone": "America/New_York"
        },
        "recurrence": ["RRULE:FREQ=WEEKLY;INTERVAL=2;BYDAY=WE"]
    });

    let resp = reqwest::Client::new()
        .post("https://www.googleapis.com/calendar/v3/calendars/primary/events")
        .bearer_auth(access_token)
        .json(&event)
        .send()
        .await?;

    let status = resp.status();
    let body: serde_json::Value = resp.json().await?;
    if !status.is_success() {
        return Err(format!("Calendar API error {status}: {body}").into());
    }

    println!("Created: {}", body["htmlLink"]);
    Ok(())
}
```

## Key details

- **`RRULE:FREQ=WEEKLY;INTERVAL=2;BYDAY=WE`** means every 2 weeks on Wednesday. The `INTERVAL=2` counts from the start date, so the first `start` should fall on the Wednesday you want as week one.
- **`timeZone` must be an IANA name** (e.g. `America/New_York`) for recurring events. Google needs it to expand the recurrence correctly across DST changes. Without it, 1pm could drift by an hour after a clock change.
- **`end` is required**, and it defines the duration of each occurrence, not the end of the series.
- **To limit the series**, append `;COUNT=10` or `;UNTIL=20270101T000000Z` to the RRULE.
- **`calendars/primary`** is your main calendar. Use a specific calendar ID to target another one.
- **For unattended or server use**, swap the installed-flow authenticator for a service account (`ServiceAccountAuthenticator`). You must then share the target calendar with the service account's email address.

## Alternative: typed client

The `google-calendar3` crate wraps the same API with typed structs (`Event`, `EventDateTime`, etc.) and uses `yup-oauth2` under the hood. It's more verbose to set up and its API shifts between versions, which is why I used raw `reqwest` above. The JSON payload is identical either way.

You can do this by calling the **Google Calendar API** `events.insert` endpoint with a recurrence rule (RRULE). The rule for "every other Wednesday" is:

```
RRULE:FREQ=WEEKLY;INTERVAL=2;BYDAY=WE
```

Below is a complete example. It uses `yup-oauth2` for authentication and `reqwest` to call the REST API.

## 1. Google Cloud setup

1. In the [Google Cloud Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/), create or select a project.
2. Enable the **Google Calendar API**.
3. Configure the **OAuth consent screen**. Add yourself as a test user.
4. Create an **OAuth client ID** of type **Desktop app**. Download the JSON file and save it as `credentials.json` in your project root.

## 2. `Cargo.toml`

```toml
[package]
name = "gcal-recurring"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"

[dependencies]
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full"] }
reqwest = { version = "0.12", features = ["json"] }
yup-oauth2 = "11"
serde_json = "1"
chrono = "0.4"
anyhow = "1"
```

## 3. `src/main.rs`

```rust
use anyhow::{Context, Result};
use chrono::{Datelike, Duration, Local, NaiveDate, Weekday};
use serde_json::json;
use yup_oauth2::{InstalledFlowAuthenticator, InstalledFlowReturnMethod};

const SCOPES: &[&str] = &["https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.events"];
const TIME_ZONE: &str = "America/New_York"; // <-- change to your IANA time zone

/// Returns the next Wednesday on or after today.
fn next_wednesday() -> NaiveDate {
    let today = Local::now().date_naive();
    let days_ahead = (Weekday::Wed.num_days_from_monday() as i64
        - today.weekday().num_days_from_monday() as i64)
        .rem_euclid(7);
    today + Duration::days(days_ahead)
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<()> {
    // --- 1. Authenticate (opens a browser the first time, then caches the token) ---
    let secret = yup_oauth2::read_application_secret("credentials.json")
        .await
        .context("reading credentials.json")?;

    let auth = InstalledFlowAuthenticator::builder(secret, InstalledFlowReturnMethod::HTTPRedirect)
        .persist_tokens_to_disk("token_cache.json")
        .build()
        .await?;

    let token = auth.token(SCOPES).await?;
    let access_token = token.token().context("no access token returned")?;

    // --- 2. Build the event ---
    // The first occurrence must itself fall on a Wednesday.
    let start_date = next_wednesday();
    let start = format!("{start_date}T13:00:00");
    let end = format!("{start_date}T14:00:00"); // 1-hour meeting

    let event = json!({
        "summary": "Biweekly Wednesday Meeting",
        "description": "Created from Rust",
        "start": { "dateTime": start, "timeZone": TIME_ZONE },
        "end":   { "dateTime": end,   "timeZone": TIME_ZONE },
        "recurrence": [
            "RRULE:FREQ=WEEKLY;INTERVAL=2;BYDAY=WE"
        ]
    });

    // --- 3. Insert it into the primary calendar ---
    let resp = reqwest::Client::new()
        .post("https://www.googleapis.com/calendar/v3/calendars/primary/events")
        .bearer_auth(access_token)
        .json(&event)
        .send()
        .await?;

    let status = resp.status();
    let body: serde_json::Value = resp.json().await?;

    if !status.is_success() {
        anyhow::bail!("Google API error {status}: {body:#}");
    }

    println!("Event created: {}", body["htmlLink"].as_str().unwrap_or("<no link>"));
    Ok(())
}
```

Run it with `cargo run`. On the first run, a browser window opens so you can grant access. After that, the token is reused from `token_cache.json`.

## Key details

- **`timeZone` is required for recurring events.** It keeps the event at 1:00 PM local time across daylight-saving changes. Because of that field, the `dateTime` values don't include a UTC offset.
- **The start date must be a Wednesday.** If it isn't, Google creates an extra first occurrence on the start date.
- **Ending the series:** append `;COUNT=10` for a fixed number of occurrences, or `;UNTIL=20271231T235959Z` for an end date. For example: `RRULE:FREQ=WEEKLY;INTERVAL=2;BYDAY=WE;COUNT=10`.
- **Which "other" Wednesday:** the two-week cycle starts from your start date. To align with a specific week, hardcode the start date, e.g. `"2026-10-14T13:00:00"`.
- **Other calendars:** replace `primary` in the URL with the calendar ID, which you can find under Calendar Settings → *Integrate calendar*.

## Alternatives

- **Typed client:** the [`google-calendar3`](https://crates.io/crates/google-calendar3) crate is generated from Google's API definitions and gives you structs like `Event` and `EventDateTime`. It's more verbose and its API changes between versions, so the raw REST approach above is often simpler.
- **Servers and automation with no browser:** use a **service account** with `yup_oauth2::ServiceAccountAuthenticator`. You also need to either share the target calendar with the service account's email or use domain-wide delegation in Google Workspace.

Here's a C program that sorts an array of integers in ascending order using bubble sort:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    int swapped;
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        swapped = 0;
        // Last i elements are already in place after each pass
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
                swapped = 1;
            }
        }
        // If no swaps occurred, the array is already sorted
        if (!swapped) {
            break;
        }
    }
}

void printArray(const int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    }
    printf("\n");
}

int main(void) {
    int arr[] = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    printf("Original array: ");
    printArray(arr, n);

    bubbleSort(arr, n);

    printf("Sorted array:   ");
    printArray(arr, n);

    return 0;
}
```

**Output:**
```
Original array: 64 34 25 12 22 11 90 
Sorted array:   11 12 22 25 34 64 90 
```

**How it works:**
- The outer loop makes up to `n - 1` passes over the array.
- The inner loop compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they're out of order, so the largest unsorted element "bubbles up" to its correct position at the end of each pass.
- The `swapped` flag lets the function stop early if a full pass makes no swaps, meaning the array is already sorted. This makes the best case O(n) instead of O(n²).

**Complexity:**
- Best case: O(n) (already sorted, thanks to the early exit)
- Average and worst case: O(n²)
- Space: O(1), since sorting happens in place

To compile and run it:
```bash
gcc -o bubble_sort bubble_sort.c
./bubble_sort
```

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

void bubble_sort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        bool swapped = false;

        // After each pass, the largest remaining element is in its final place
        for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                int tmp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = tmp;
                swapped = true;
            }
        }

        // No swaps means the array is already sorted
        if (!swapped) break;
    }
}

void print_array(const int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    }
    printf("\n");
}

int main(void) {
    int arr[] = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90, -5, 0};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    printf("Before: ");
    print_array(arr, n);

    bubble_sort(arr, n);

    printf("After:  ");
    print_array(arr, n);

    return 0;
}
```

