## How it works

**Behavior**
- **Collapsed:** The header (with a `+` square), the intro, and an "expand more" button are shown.
- **Expanded:** The header shows `−`, the intro stays visible, the details appear, and the "expand more" button is hidden.
- Clicking the header toggles between the two states. Clicking "expand more" expands the component.

**API, similar to `<details>`**
- The `open` attribute sets or reflects the state, e.g. `<x-details open>`.
- The `el.open = true/false` property and the `el.toggle()` method change the state from JavaScript.
- A `toggle` event fires whenever the state changes:
  ```js
  document.querySelector('x-details')
    .addEventListener('toggle', e => console.log(e.target.open));
  ```

**Accessibility**
- The header is a real `<button>`, so it works with the keyboard (Tab, Enter, Space).
- The header has `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls` attributes, so screen readers announce whether it is expanded.
- After "expand more" is clicked, it disappears. Focus moves to the header so keyboard users don't lose their place.

**Styling from outside**
The internal parts are exposed with `::part()`, so you can restyle them without editing the component:
```css
x-details::part(header) { border-bottom-color: steelblue; }
x-details::part(marker) { border-radius: 3px; }
x-details::part(expand-button) { background: #eee; border: 1px solid #aaa; }
```

# Controlling Two DC Motors with an L298N on Ubuntu (C++)

This uses **libgpiod**, the current standard way to control GPIO pins on Linux. It assumes Ubuntu is running on a board with GPIO pins, such as a **Raspberry Pi**. A regular desktop PC has no GPIO header, so it can't drive the L298N directly.

---

## 1. Wiring

| L298N Pin | Connects To | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| **ENA** | GPIO 24 (physical pin 18) | Enable Motor A (remove the ENA jumper first) |
| **IN1** | GPIO 17 (physical pin 11) | Motor A direction input 1 |
| **IN2** | GPIO 27 (physical pin 13) | Motor A direction input 2 |
| **IN3** | GPIO 22 (physical pin 15) | Motor B direction input 1 |
| **IN4** | GPIO 23 (physical pin 16) | Motor B direction input 2 |
| **ENB** | GPIO 25 (physical pin 22) | Enable Motor B (remove the ENB jumper first) |
| **GND** | Pi GND (physical pin 6) **and** battery negative | Common ground is required |
| **+12V / VS** | Battery positive (~7–9V) | Motor power |
| **OUT1 / OUT2** | Motor A leads | |
| **OUT3 / OUT4** | Motor B leads | |

**Hardware notes:**
- **Never power the motors from the Pi's 5V pin.** Use a separate battery pack.
- The L298N loses about **2V** internally. With 5V motors, a supply of about **7V** (for example, 6×AA) gives the motors roughly 5V.
- The Pi's 3.3V logic is high enough for the L298N's IN/EN pins.

### How the L298N decides what a motor does

| EN | IN1 | IN2 | Result |
|---|---|---|---|
| HIGH | HIGH | LOW | Forward |
| HIGH | LOW | HIGH | Reverse |
| HIGH | LOW | LOW | Brake (fast stop) |
| LOW | any | any | Coast (free spin stop) |

Motor B works the same way using IN3/IN4 with ENB.

---

## 2. Install the Library

```bash
sudo apt update                      # Refresh the package list
sudo apt install libgpiod-dev gpiod  # Install the GPIO library and command-line tools
gpiodetect                           # Lists GPIO chips; note which one is the main header (usually gpiochip0)
apt show libgpiod-dev | grep Version # The code below requires version 2.x (Ubuntu 24.04 and newer)
```

> On a **Raspberry Pi 5** with some older kernels, the header pins are on `gpiochip4` instead of `gpiochip0`. Use `gpioinfo` to check.

---

## 3. The Code: `motors.cpp`

```cpp
#include <gpiod.h>      // The libgpiod library: lets us control GPIO pins from Linux
#include <iostream>     // Lets us print messages with std::cout and std::cerr
#include <thread>       // Gives us std::this_thread::sleep_for to pause the program
#include <chrono>       // Gives us time units like std::chrono::seconds

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PIN DEFINITIONS - these are BCM GPIO numbers (not physical pin numbers)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
const unsigned int ENA = 24;  // GPIO 24 -> L298N ENA (turns Motor A on/off)
const unsigned int IN1 = 17;  // GPIO 17 -> L298N IN1 (Motor A direction bit 1)
const unsigned int IN2 = 27;  // GPIO 27 -> L298N IN2 (Motor A direction bit 2)
const unsigned int IN3 = 22;  // GPIO 22 -> L298N IN3 (Motor B direction bit 1)
const unsigned int IN4 = 23;  // GPIO 23 -> L298N IN4 (Motor B direction bit 2)
const unsigned int ENB = 25;  // GPIO 25 -> L298N ENB (turns Motor B on/off)

// Short names for the two pin states, so the code reads more clearly
const gpiod_line_value HIGH = GPIOD_LINE_VALUE_ACTIVE;    // HIGH = 3.3V on the pin
const gpiod_line_value LOW  = GPIOD_LINE_VALUE_INACTIVE;  // LOW  = 0V on the pin

int main()                                                 // Program starts running here
{
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 1: Open the GPIO chip (the hardware that owns the header pins)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    gpiod_chip *chip = gpiod_chip_open("/dev/gpiochip0");  // Open the GPIO controller device file
    if (chip == nullptr) {                                 // nullptr means opening failed
        std::cerr << "Could not open gpiochip0\n";         // Print an error message
        return 1;                                          // Exit; a non-zero value means "error"
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 2: Describe HOW we want the pins set up
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    gpiod_line_settings *settings = gpiod_line_settings_new();                // Create an empty settings object
    gpiod_line_settings_set_direction(settings, GPIOD_LINE_DIRECTION_OUTPUT); // Pins will be OUTPUTS (we send signals out)
    gpiod_line_settings_set_output_value(settings, LOW);                      // Start every pin LOW so motors are off at startup

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 3: Describe WHICH pins get those settings
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    unsigned int pins[6] = { ENA, IN1, IN2, IN3, IN4, ENB };   // Array of all six pins we will control
    gpiod_line_config *line_cfg = gpiod_line_config_new();     // Create an empty line-configuration object
    gpiod_line_config_add_line_settings(line_cfg, pins, 6, settings); // Apply "output, start LOW" to all 6 pins

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 4: Name our request and ask Linux for control of the pins
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    gpiod_request_config *req_cfg = gpiod_request_config_new(); // Create a request-configuration object
    gpiod_request_config_set_consumer(req_cfg, "l298n_motors"); // Label shown in `gpioinfo` so you can see who owns the pins

    gpiod_line_request *motors = gpiod_chip_request_lines(chip, req_cfg, line_cfg); // Actually claim the pins
    if (motors == nullptr) {                                    // nullptr means the request failed
        std::cerr << "Could not claim GPIO lines (try sudo?)\n";// Common cause: missing permissions
        gpiod_chip_close(chip);                                 // Close the chip before exiting
        return 1;                                               // Exit with an error code
    }

    std::cout << "Pins ready.\n";                               // Confirm setup succeeded

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 5: Enable both motor channels (EN pins HIGH = "allowed to run")
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, ENA, HIGH);            // Turn on the Motor A channel
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, ENB, HIGH);            // Turn on the Motor B channel

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 6: BOTH MOTORS FORWARD
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    std::cout << "Forward\n";                                   // Report what is happening
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN1, HIGH);            // Motor A: IN1 HIGH...
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN2, LOW);             // ...and IN2 LOW = forward
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN3, HIGH);            // Motor B: IN3 HIGH...
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN4, LOW);             // ...and IN4 LOW = forward
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(2));       // Let the motors run for 2 seconds

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 7: BRAKE (both IN pins LOW while EN is HIGH = fast stop)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    std::cout << "Stop\n";                                      // Report what is happening
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN1, LOW);             // Motor A: IN1 LOW...
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN2, LOW);             // ...and IN2 LOW = brake
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN3, LOW);             // Motor B: IN3 LOW...
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN4, LOW);             // ...and IN4 LOW = brake
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));       // Pause 1 second; reversing instantly strains the motors

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 8: BOTH MOTORS REVERSE (swap the HIGH/LOW pattern)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    std::cout << "Reverse\n";                                   // Report what is happening
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN1, LOW);             // Motor A: IN1 LOW...
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN2, HIGH);            // ...and IN2 HIGH = reverse
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN3, LOW);             // Motor B: IN3 LOW...
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN4, HIGH);            // ...and IN4 HIGH = reverse
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(2));       // Run in reverse for 2 seconds

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 9: BRAKE again
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    std::cout << "Stop\n";                                      // Report what is happening
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN1, LOW);             // Motor A: brake
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN2, LOW);             // Motor A: brake
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN3, LOW);             // Motor B: brake
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN4, LOW);             // Motor B: brake
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));       // Pause 1 second

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 10: SPIN IN PLACE (motors turn opposite directions, like a tank)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    std::cout << "Spin\n";                                      // Report what is happening
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN1, HIGH);            // Motor A forward (IN1 HIGH...)
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN2, LOW);             // (...IN2 LOW)
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN3, LOW);             // Motor B reverse (IN3 LOW...)
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN4, HIGH);            // (...IN4 HIGH)
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));       // Spin for 1 second

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 11: FINAL STOP - coast by turning the enable pins off
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    std::cout << "Coast to stop\n";                             // Report what is happening
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, ENA, LOW);             // Disable Motor A (coasts freely)
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, ENB, LOW);             // Disable Motor B (coasts freely)
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN1, LOW);             // Leave all direction pins LOW...
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN2, LOW);             // ...so nothing starts unexpectedly
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN3, LOW);             // ...
    gpiod_line_request_set_value(motors, IN4, LOW);             // ...

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // STEP 12: CLEAN UP - give everything back to Linux
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    gpiod_line_request_release(motors);        // Release the pins so other programs can use them
    gpiod_request_config_free(req_cfg);        // Free the memory used by the request config
    gpiod_line_config_free(line_cfg);          // Free the memory used by the line config
    gpiod_line_settings_free(settings);        // Free the memory used by the settings
    gpiod_chip_close(chip);                    // Close the GPIO chip device

    std::cout << "Done.\n";                    // Report that the program finished
    return 0;                                  // Exit; 0 means "success"
}
```

