## 5. Exercises to Learn From

The code is written out the long way on purpose. These changes will build your skills:

1. **Write functions.** Steps 7 and 9 are identical. Turn them into a `void brake(gpiod_line_request *m)` function. Then write `forward()`, `reverse()`, and `spin()`.
2. **Add a loop.** Use a `for` loop to repeat the forward/reverse cycle 3 times.
3. **Use arrays and loops together.** Put IN1–IN4 in an array and loop over it to set them all LOW.
4. **Add speed control (PWM).** Switching ENA HIGH and LOW quickly in a loop varies the speed. For example, 10 ms on and 10 ms off gives about 50% speed. That's "software PWM." Later, look into the Pi's **hardware PWM** (`/sys/class/pwm`) for smoother control.
5. **Add safe shutdown.** Look up `std::signal(SIGINT, ...)` so pressing Ctrl+C stops the motors instead of leaving them running.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int t;
    scanf("%d", &t);
    while (t--) {
        int n, k;
        scanf("%d %d", &n, &k);
        vector<int> a(n);
        for (auto &x : a) scanf("%d", &x);

        vector<int> dp(n);
        auto check = [&](int x) -> bool {
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                int b = a[i] >= x ? 1 : -1;
                if (i % k == 0) {
                    dp[i] = b;
                    if (i >= k) dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[i - k]);
                } else {
                    dp[i] = dp[i - 1] + b;
                    if (i >= k) dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[i - k]);
                }
            }
            return dp[n - 1] > 0;
        };

        int lo = 1, hi = 1000000000;
        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo + 1) / 2;
            if (check(mid)) lo = mid;
            else hi = mid - 1;
        }
        printf("%d\n", lo);
    }
    return 0;
}
